# ==========================================================================
#  O44  Existential restrictions: a necessary condition, and the open world
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    'Every bookshop is located in some settlement' is written as a
#    subclass axiom with owl:someValuesFrom, which constrains the set
#    without producing a triple, and a shop with no recorded town is not an
#    error.
#
#  WHY
#    A restriction is a set defined by a condition: the things that have
#    some bs:locatedIn value in bs:Settlement. Writing bs:Bookshop
#    rdfs:subClassOf that set says every bookshop is such a thing. It is a
#    necessary condition: to be a bookshop you must be located in a
#    settlement. It is not sufficient, because other things are located in
#    settlements too, and it is not a defined class; o46 is that.
#
#    What does the reasoner do with it? Less than newcomers expect. Given a
#    bookshop with no recorded town, it does not complain: the open world
#    says the town exists and is not in the file. Given a bookshop located
#    in something, it does not type the something as a Settlement, because
#    the axiom says some value is a settlement, not every value. HermiT
#    reports this file consistent and infers nothing visible, and that is
#    correct. The axiom has narrowed the set of interpretations, which is
#    what an axiom does, and nothing in the data happened to be decided by
#    it.
#
#    That is why gist's rules say to use someValuesFrom in an equivalence
#    when you want to infer into a class, and why the shape that says
#    'every shop must have a town in this dataset' is SHACL, not this. The
#    SHACL course's s04 is that shape, and it reports the shop with no
#    town; this axiom never will.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       bs:Bookshop rdfs:subClassOf [ owl:onProperty bs:locatedIn ; owl:someValuesFrom bs:Settlement ]
#
#       set reading:  B is a subset of { x : some y, locatedIn(x, y), y in S }
#
#       a shop with no town in the file      consistent: the town exists, unrecorded (open world)
#       a shop located in something          the something is not typed: 'some', not 'every'
#       HermiT: consistent, nothing new      correct, and the point
#
#       want to find shops with no town?     a shape: sh:path bs:locatedIn ; sh:minCount 1   (SHACL course s04)
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - A subclass existential is a necessary condition on the set. It
#      rejects nothing and types nothing; it narrows what the world could
#      be.
#    - Absence is not error. The open world says the missing value exists
#      and is unrecorded.
#    - To find the missing value, write a shape. To infer membership from
#      a value, use an equivalence (o46).
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F07-owl%2Fo44-existential-restrictions-a-necessary-condition-and-the-open-world.ttl
#  SOURCES   OWL 2 Primer 5.2 Property Restrictions: existential quantification
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/#Property_Restrictions
#            OWL 2 Primer 2.2 Modeling Knowledge: Basic Notions (the open world, no unique names)
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/#Modeling_Knowledge:_Basic_Notions
#  MEASURED  parses, 14 triples; 2 classes, 1 object property, 2 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; HermiT: consistent, nothing new; RL closure +0; 'Shops with no recorded town ' 1 row
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bs:Settlement  a owl:Class .
bs:Bookshop    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty bs:locatedIn ; owl:someValuesFrom bs:Settlement ] .
bs:locatedIn   a owl:ObjectProperty .

bt:shop-inkwell   a bs:Bookshop ; bs:locatedIn bt:place-wigtown .   # the town's type is not inferred
bt:shop-errata    a bs:Bookshop .                                   # no town recorded: not an error
bt:place-wigtown  skos:prefLabel "Wigtown"@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Shops with no recorded town (what a shape would report)  (on the model)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  SELECT ?shop WHERE { ?shop a bs:Bookshop . FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?shop bs:locatedIn ?town } }
